 he gets in his toyota and he he  wants to go get something to eat but  instead of going to get something he  just gets in the toyota says well i  guess i'm finished right i finally got  there you didn't get anywhere right it's  merely it's going to take you where you  want to go and the question is then  what is my arrival point where do i want  to go and how will minimalism serve me  on that journey because otherwise it  just becomes about getting rid of the  stuff and then you'll you'll never get  though you'll turn into a spartanist  which is the opposite of a hoarder we  did an episode about this recently so  spartanus for those of you who are  uninformed is just an  obsessive-compulsive declutterer  basically  and so it's a person who can't stop  letting go  well that's we want to avoid that as  well both of these people are on the  same spectrum hoarders can't let go of  anything a spartanness can't hold on to  anything a minimalist is on a different  spectrum altogether it's the  intentionality spectrum and you can be a  minimalist like me and ryan or you can  go even farther and you can be a a monk  or or an ascetic right someone who lives  with very few items but does so  intentionally or you could even be a  maximalist there are some great  maximalist designers out there who  use objects in a way that is very  intentional it would be clutter for me  but it serves great purpose for them the  question is how am i going to live my  life  so it gets me where i want to go  if you don't know where you want to go i  can promise you you won't get there  oh  um  i'm trying to say this in the kindness  kind of way you'll you'll  there is no there is no finish line with  that with the decluttering projects now  there might be  a certain  thing that's happening where you're  finally like tidying up everything and  that's incredible and i find that you  know i am constantly i get tidied up and  then you know all the you know the [ __ ]  the junk drawer starts to become two  drunk drawers you're like oh no like i  have to do this tidying up thing again  but you know minimalists they don't  focus on  less less less they focus on  more it's creating more time  it's creating more freedom more  creativity more experiences so the  question is is what are you freeing up  all that time for what are you freeing  up that space for and maybe you don't  have an answer for that that's okay  but that's really  where  where i was drawn to minimalism i didn't  see it as like a as a magical bullet  answer was like oh if i throw all my  stuff away i'm gonna immediately start  experiencing perpetual bliss like anyone  could do that and still be miserable  right  for me it was regaining control of my  finances  it was regaining control of my time  it was making it so i didn't have to  uh  work 60 70 80 hours a week to pay off  you know all that debt that i have  accumulated  so  yeah i would ask yourself what are you  freeing up all of this space for the the  literal and  the metaphorical space what are you  freeing all that up for  it's beautiful  thank you thank you thank you so much  did you enjoy this video if so you can  listen to full episodes of the  minimalist private podcast available  exclusively on patreon visit patreon.com  the minimalists today your support keeps  our podcast and youtube channel one  hundred percent advertisement free

